WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.160
AI workloads are often constrained by specialized resources, not just ordinary CPU and memory.

2
00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:13.740
A service may be blocked by GPU availability, accelerator memory, storage throughput, network transfer, or scheduler decisions.

3
00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:20.150
Resource telemetry helps teams separate model behavior problems from capacity, placement, and infrastructure problems.

4
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:29.290
At the node level, teams usually need CPU, memory, disk, network, process health, file system pressure, and container or pod status.

5
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:33.750
For AI workloads, accelerator signals become just as important.

6
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:42.890
Useful GPU and accelerator telemetry may include utilization, memory usage, temperature, power draw, throttling, device errors, driver health, and device availability.

7
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:51.250
These signals help explain why a model path may slow down even when application logs show no obvious errors.

8
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.710
Cluster telemetry should show scheduler pressure and workload placement.

9
00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:05.240
Pending jobs, failed placements, evictions, queue depth, preemption, resource requests, resource limits, node labels, and capacity trends all affect AI service behavior.

10
00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:13.990
A model-serving endpoint may fail an SLO because requests wait behind other work, not because the model itself changed.

11
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.280
Storage and network telemetry also matter.

12
00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:23.690
Model loading, embedding indexes, checkpoints, training data, retrieval sources, and logs can create bottlenecks.

13
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:31.660
Historical telemetry supports capacity planning by showing real patterns across peak traffic, batch windows, release events, and incidents.

14
00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:38.850
Without that history, teams often guess, overprovision, or chase symptoms instead of fixing the constrained layer.

